{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And he ate, and he drank, and he rose, and he went, and he despised.</b> In these five words there is an allusion to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Bava Batra 16b), who said: five transgressions did that wicked man commit on that day, etc. 'And he ate' — this is sexual immorality, as it is written (Exodus 2:20) ״Call him, that he may eat bread,״ ״save the bread which he eats״ (Genesis 39:6). 'And he drank' — this is that he spilled blood, as it is written (Numbers 23:24) ״and the blood of the slain he shall drink.״ 'And he rose' — that he denied the fundamental principle [of faith], as it is written (Deuteronomy 31:16) ״and the people will rise and go astray after the foreign gods of the land,״ and the expression 'and he rose' is apt here, for one who walks with an upright posture is as though he pushes away the feet of the Shekhinah (Berakhot 43b). 'And he went' — that he denied the resurrection of the dead, as he said, ״Behold, I am going to die״ etc. (Genesis 25:32), and so Job said (Job 7:9) ״A cloud dissolves and goes — so one who descends to Sheol does not rise.״ 'And he despised' — this is that he mocked the birthright and despised the service of HaShem. And some say 'and he rose and went' means he walked here and there, to show that he was healthy, so that his sale should not be like the sale of one mortally ill."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:15.490191039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}